        "readable_text": "Content:\nNow that we know how to compute powers of matrices, we can evaluate polynomials where the input is a square matrix.\n\nContent:\nFor example, consider the polynomial\n\nContent:\n[MATH: f(X) = X^2 + 4X + 3.]\n\nContent:\nNote that, in order to evaluate this polynomial for an input matrix [MATH: X,] we need to interpret any constants as multiples of the identity matrix. So, we interpret the polynomial as\n\nContent:\n[MATH: f(X) = X^2 + 4X + 3I.]\n\nContent:\nFor example, if [MATH: [1|1; 0|2]] then\n\nContent:\n[MATH: A^{2}|= [1|1; 0|2][1|1; 0|2]; = [1|3; 0|4],]\n\nContent:\nand we can evaluate [MATH: f(A)] as follows:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: f(A)|= A^{2} + 4A + 3I; = [1|3; 0|4] + 4[1|1; 0|2] + 3[1|0; 0|1]; = [1|3; 0|4] + [4|4; 0|8] + [3|0; 0|3]; = [1 + 4 + 3|3 + 4 + 0; 0 + 0 + 0|4 + 8 + 3]; = [8|7; 0|15]]"
      },
